@event club_porter
# en: The Porter's Lodge
title [fa0c8d] = 门房
# en: {&The club's porter looks at your boots, then your face, then your boots again. "Members and their guests," he says. Beyond the inner door are leather chairs, low lamps and the sort of people whose names are in the newspapers on purpose.|The porter again. He has decided, it seems, that you're a recurring weather condition. "Members and their guests," he says, with less conviction.|"You again," says the porter, almost warmly. Through the inner door you can hear a minister losing at cards.}
text [ac2429] = {&俱乐部的门房看看你的靴子，又看看你的脸，再看看你的靴子。“只限会员及其宾客，”他说。里间的门后是皮椅、低垂的灯，还有那种故意让名字上报纸的人。|又是那位门房。看来他已经认定你是一种反复出现的天气。“只限会员及其宾客，”他说，底气少了些。|“又是你，”门房说，几乎有点亲切。透过里间的门，你能听见一位大臣在牌桌上输钱。}
# en: A members' club's marble hall, a stately porter in a braided coat at his lodge window looking a student up and down, leather chairs and lamplight beyond a half-open door.
scene [f5cc60] = 会员俱乐部的大理石门厅，一位身穿镶边外套、仪表堂堂的门房从门房窗口上下打量一个学生，半开的门后是皮椅和灯光。
# en: Talk your way in as somebody's guest
opt.left.label [dace9a] = 冒充某位会员的宾客混进去
# en: {&You name a member you read about in the paper, with total confidence, and the porter waves you through. You spend hours in a leather chair overhearing two bankers decide the price of bread. Nobody asks who you are. Everyone nods.|You say you're expected by "the admiral", and there is, it turns out, an admiral, asleep. You sit near him for hours, a guest in good standing.|This time you just walk in as if you'd always been a member. The porter, defeated, takes your coat. A duchess asks your opinion on a war.}
opt.left.say.1 [1fe43b] = {&你满怀自信地报出一个在报纸上读到的会员名字，门房挥手放你进去。你在一把皮椅上坐了几个小时，偷听两位银行家决定面包的价钱。没人问你是谁。人人都冲你点头。|你说是“那位海军上将”约的你，结果还真有一位海军上将，正睡着。你在他旁边坐了几个小时，俨然一位体面的宾客。|这回你干脆像个老会员一样径直走进去。门房认了输，替你接过外套。一位公爵夫人问你对一场战争有何高见。}
# en: {&The member you name died last winter. The porter tells you so, kindly, and shows you the street.|"The admiral," says the porter, "is in the next room, and has never heard of you. I asked." You leave with your ears hot.|The porter doesn't even look up. "No," he says. You go.}
opt.left.say.2 [c46565] = {&你报的那位会员去年冬天就去世了。门房和气地告诉你这件事，然后把你请回了街上。|“那位海军上将，”门房说，“就在隔壁房间，从没听说过你。我问过了。”你耳根发烫地离开了。|门房连头都没抬。“不行，”他说。你走了。}
# en: Sit in the lodge and let him talk
opt.right.label [faa132] = 坐进门房小屋，听他说
# en: {&The porter, it turns out, knows everything about everyone in the building, and has been waiting thirty years for someone to tell. An hour later you know who owes whom, who isn't speaking to whom and who is wearing a wig. Your ears ache.|The porter has a stool by his lodge fire and a fresh list of scandals. You learn how a general cheats at cards and how an archdeacon tips. It's a long hour.|He pours you a cup from his own pot and tells you which members are about to be ruined. You suspect he's guessing. He's never wrong. You leave weighed down by it.}
opt.right.say.1 [e45894] = {&原来门房对这栋楼里每个人的事都了如指掌，等了三十年就等有人来听。一个小时后，你知道了谁欠谁的钱、谁和谁不说话、谁戴着假发。你的耳朵都累了。|门房的炉火边有张凳子，他手里有一份新鲜的丑闻清单。你知道了一位将军怎么在牌桌上作弊、一位副主教怎么给小费。这一个小时很漫长。|他用自己的壶给你倒了一杯，告诉你哪些会员快要破产了。你怀疑他是猜的。他从没猜错过。你离开时心头沉甸甸的。}

@event club_card_room
# en: Stakes of Another Kind
title [e9f53a] = 另一种赌注
# en: In the club's card room, a retired general with one sleeve pinned up is short of a fourth at his table. He doesn't play for crowns, he tells you, he has enough of those. He plays for favours. "If you win, I'll owe you one, and I'm worth owing. If you lose, you'll owe me one. I collect at once."
text [f3afa5] = 俱乐部的牌室里，一位一只袖管别起来的退役将军牌桌上缺一个人。他说他不赌克朗，克朗他够多了。他赌人情。“你赢了，我欠你一个人情，我的人情很值钱。你输了，你欠我一个。我当场就讨。”
# en: A green-baize card room in a members' club, a white-moustached retired general with one empty sleeve pinned up dealing one-handed at a corner table, an empty chair opposite him.
scene [2b337b] = 会员俱乐部铺着绿呢的牌室，一位白胡子的退役将军别着一只空袖管，在角落的桌子上单手发牌，对面空着一把椅子。
# en: Take the empty chair
opt.left.label [2c28bd] = 坐上那把空椅子
# en: You hold your nerve through four rubbers and take the last trick with a card he didn't know you had. The general laughs and pays his debt at once, his way: he pays your table share himself, then stands and tells the whole card room how you did it. "Paid," he says. "People will hear about it."
opt.left.say.1 [3a5d4d] = 你稳住神打了四局，最后一墩用一张他没料到你有的牌拿下。将军大笑，当场用他的方式还了这份人情：亲自替你付了牌桌的份钱，然后站起来，把你怎么赢的讲给整个牌室听。“还清了，”他说，“大家会听说这件事的。”
# en: He takes the last trick with a card you didn't know he had. "You owe me a favour," he says, "and I collect at once." The favour is reading his memoirs aloud to him till the club shuts, all four campaigns. You go home hoarse, at two in the morning.
opt.left.say.2 [962d40] = 他用一张你没料到他有的牌拿下最后一墩。“你欠我一个人情，”他说，“我当场就讨。”这个人情是给他朗读回忆录，一直读到俱乐部打烊，整整四场战役。你凌晨两点才回到家，嗓子哑了。
# en: Thank him and stay a spectator
opt.right.label [2f1e47] = 谢过他，只做个旁观者
# en: You watch from a chair by the fire as he wins a promise from a magistrate, a horse from a shipowner and a vote from an alderman, smiling throughout. It's the most frightening thing you've seen in the capital, and you learn a great deal.
opt.right.say.1 [6ef9e2] = 你坐在炉火边的椅子上看着，看他从一位治安法官那里赢走一个承诺，从一位船东那里赢走一匹马，从一位市参议员那里赢走一张选票，自始至终面带微笑。这是你在王都见过最吓人的事，你也学到了很多。

@event club_blackball
# en: White and Black
title [9f2298] = 白球与黑球
# en: The club is voting on a new member: a shipbuilder who made his money with his own hands, the members murmur, as if that were catching. The ballot box goes round the room, and an elderly member, deaf and in a hurry, presses a white ball and a black one into your hand, taking you for somebody's nephew. One ball in the box. Nobody will ever know which.
text [d6788d] = 俱乐部正在表决一位新会员：一个靠自己双手发家的造船商——会员们嘀咕着，仿佛这会传染。投票箱在屋里传了一圈，一位耳背又匆忙的老会员把你当成了谁家的侄子，往你手里塞了一颗白球和一颗黑球。投一颗进箱子。谁也不会知道是哪颗。
# en: A members' club committee room, a mahogany ballot box, a bowl of white and black balls, an elderly member handing one of each to a student by mistake.
scene [01d174] = 会员俱乐部的委员会议室，一只桃花心木投票箱，一碗黑白小球，一位年长会员错把一黑一白两颗球递给一个学生。
# en: Drop in the white ball
opt.left.label [f65efa] = 投进白球
# en: The white ball drops with a little click. When the count is read he's in by one, and a red-faced member demands a recount, and stares about the room for the traitor. You keep your face very still. You were the one.
opt.left.say.1 [a663a0] = 白球咔哒一声落下。计票结果宣布，他以一票之差入选，一个面红耳赤的会员要求重新计票，在屋里四下怒视找叛徒。你绷紧了脸。那个人就是你。
# en: Drop in the black ball
opt.right.label [0335e8] = 投进黑球
# en: The black ball drops. He's kept out by one. The members are delighted with each other, and one claps you on the shoulder and asks you to dine, taking you for somebody's nephew still, and tells the room your name. You walk home past the shipyards and can't look at them.
opt.right.say.1 [83a9fc] = 黑球落下。他以一票之差落选。会员们相互道贺，其中一位拍了拍你的肩，请你留下吃饭——他还把你当成谁家的侄子——又向满屋子的人报了你的名字。你走回家时经过造船厂，不敢看它们。

@event club_reading_room
# en: The Explorer in the Reading Room
title [540c70] = 阅览室里的探险家
# en: In the club's reading room, where nobody may speak above a whisper, a very old explorer beckons you over to the globe with his stick. He's been everywhere, he whispers, and nobody here will listen any more. He spins the globe and stops it with one finger on a blank patch of ocean. "There's something there," he whispers. "I'll tell you about it. It'll take a while."
text [a20aba] = 俱乐部的阅览室里谁也不许高声说话，一位非常老的探险家用手杖招呼你到地球仪旁边。他低声说，他哪儿都去过，可这儿再没人愿意听了。他转动地球仪，一根手指按停在一片空白的海洋上。“那里有东西，”他低声说，“我讲给你听。得讲一阵子。”
# en: A hushed club reading room of leather armchairs and tall windows, a very old explorer with a white beard and a stick beckoning a student towards a globe.
scene [ead496] = 安静的俱乐部阅览室，皮扶手椅和高窗，一位白胡子拄手杖的老探险家正招呼一个学生到地球仪旁。
# en: Let him tell you
opt.left.label [f25f4c] = 让他讲
# en: It takes all afternoon, in whispers, standing, because he doesn't sit. An island that wasn't on the charts, a lighthouse with no keeper, a song from under the water. By the end your legs ache, your head is full of salt, and the reading room has fallen asleep around you.
opt.left.say.1 [d07918] = 讲了整整一下午，全是低语，而且站着，因为他不坐。一座海图上没有的岛，一座没有看守人的灯塔，一支从水下传来的歌。到最后你腿酸了，满脑子都是盐，整个阅览室在你们周围睡着了。
# en: Ask him what he'd do next
opt.right.label [15e66c] = 问他接下来想做什么
# en: Nobody has asked him that in years. He thinks for a long time, then whispers a list: a mountain, a river mouth, a woman in a port he should have married. You help him write it out in a fair hand. He folds it into his waistcoat. You walk out a little sad, and he walks out lighter.
opt.right.say.1 [1c137e] = 已经好多年没人这么问他了。他想了很久，然后低声说出一张清单：一座山，一个河口，一个他本该娶的港口女人。你帮他用工整的字写了下来。他把它折进背心口袋。你离开时有点难过，他离开时却轻松了些。

@event theatre_the_gods
# en: Up in the Gods
title [d6c16b] = 楼顶的廉价座
# en: {&The cheapest seats in the theatre are up in the gods: benches under the roof, where the crowd shouts advice to the actors and throws nuts at the villain. Tonight it's a tragedy about a king who loses everything. The gods are on his side, loudly.|Up in the gods again. Tonight it's a comedy about twins, and the gallery is already ahead of the plot.|The gods are packed for a melodrama about a shipwreck, with real water. The front row has brought umbrellas.}
text [ed86c0] = {&剧院最便宜的座位在楼顶的“神座”：屋顶下的长凳，观众在那儿冲演员喊建议，往反派身上扔坚果。今晚演的是一个国王失去一切的悲剧。神座站在他那边，嗓门很大。|又上了神座。今晚是一出双胞胎喜剧，楼座的观众已经抢在剧情前头了。|神座挤满了人，来看一出用真水的海难情节剧。前排的人带了伞。}
# en: The steep top gallery of a gilded theatre, crowded benches of rowdy theatregoers leaning over the rail, a stage far below, a student squeezed among them.
scene [b835e7] = 镀金剧院陡峭的顶层楼座，挤满吵闹观众的长凳，人们探身在栏杆外，舞台远在下方，一个学生夹在其中。
# en: Pay for a bench
opt.left.label [065977] = 买一张长凳票
# en: {&You cry when the king dies and boo his daughters, and so does everyone in the gods, and the actor playing the king bows to the gallery last, as if you'd been the only ones watching.|You laugh till you ache. The gallery guesses the twist an act early and cheers the actors anyway when they get there.|The water comes over the stage rail in the second act and over the front row in the third. You come out damp and delighted.}
opt.left.say.1 [4df587] = {&国王死时你哭了，你嘘他的女儿们，神座里人人如此，扮演国王的演员最后一个向楼座鞠躬，仿佛只有你们在看。|你笑到肚子疼。楼座提前一幕猜到了反转，演员们演到那里时大家照样为他们欢呼。|第二幕水漫过了舞台栏杆，第三幕漫过了前排。你出来时湿漉漉的，开心极了。}
# en: Slip in at the interval
opt.right.label [db8761] = 趁幕间溜进去
# en: {&At the interval you walk in backwards among the smokers coming out, as if you were leaving. It works. You see the second half free, standing, and feel only slightly like a thief.|You come in with the orange-sellers, holding a borrowed tray. The usher waves you through. You sell two oranges by accident.|You tag along behind a party of sailors who are very sure they've already paid. So, now, are you.}
opt.right.say.1 [59abe9] = {&幕间休息时，你倒着走进出来抽烟的人群里，好像正要离开。成了。你站着免费看完了下半场，只稍微觉得自己像个小偷。|你跟着卖橘子的人进去，手里端着一只借来的托盘。引座员挥手放行。你还不小心卖掉了两个橘子。|你跟在一群笃定自己已经买过票的水手后面。现在你也笃定了。}

@event theatre_vey_ghost
# en: The Ghost Has the Mumps
title [59e60c] = 幽灵得了腮腺炎
# en: Backstage, Professor Vey grabs your sleeve. She spends every break on the theatre's season, it turns out, and the ghost in tonight's tragedy has the mumps. "You," she says. "You can hold a glamour. Can you hold it for three hours, in front of eleven hundred people, and walk through a wall at the end of act two? Don't answer. You can."
text [0a5e80] = 后台，维伊教授一把抓住你的袖子。原来她每个假期都在忙剧院的演出季，而今晚悲剧里的幽灵得了腮腺炎。“你，”她说，“你能维持一个魅影。你能在一千一百人面前维持三个小时，并且在第二幕末尾穿墙而过吗？别回答。你能。”
# en: A theatre's backstage corridor at curtain-up, a sharp, amused professor in a velvet coat with a script under her arm grabbing a student by the sleeve, costumed actors rushing past.
scene [cbd86a] = 开幕时分剧院的后台走廊，一位穿天鹅绒外套、腋下夹着剧本、神情敏锐而带笑的教授一把抓住一个学生的袖子，身着戏服的演员匆匆跑过。
# en: Go on as the ghost
opt.left.label [8fea78] = 扮幽灵上台
# en: You hold the glamour for three hours, pale and see-through, and walk through the wall on cue, and the gods scream. At the curtain Vey is delighted. "I was watching for the join all night," she says, "and I never found it." The papers mention the ghost.
opt.left.say.1 [3a76b6] = 你维持了三个小时的魅影，苍白透明，按点穿墙而过，神座一片尖叫。谢幕时维伊高兴极了。“我整晚都在找接缝，”她说，“一次也没找到。”报纸提到了那个幽灵。
# en: The glamour slips in act two and the ghost briefly has your face and your boots. The gods love it. Vey, in the wings, laughs until she has to sit on a costume trunk. "Next season," she says, wiping her eyes, "we'll write that in."
opt.left.say.2 [a98b3f] = 第二幕时魅影滑落了，幽灵一度长着你的脸、穿着你的靴子。神座爱死了。维伊在侧幕笑得只好坐到一只戏服箱上。“下个演出季，”她擦着眼泪说，“我们把这段写进去。”
# en: Watch from her box instead
opt.right.label [1feccc] = 改在她的包厢里看
# en: You watch from Vey's box while she whispers every trick of the trade, how the wall works, why the gods scream, where the ghost stood when the glamour faltered. The understudy's ghost is dreadful. You wish, by the end, that you'd said yes.
opt.right.say.1 [3cc36b] = 你在维伊的包厢里看戏，她悄声讲着这一行的每个诀窍：墙是怎么回事，神座为什么尖叫，魅影出岔子时幽灵站在哪儿。替补的幽灵糟透了。到最后，你真希望自己当时答应了。

@event theatre_opera_glasses
# en: The Boxes Watch the Boxes
title [5ca1bc] = 包厢在看包厢
# en: An usher offers you an empty box for a coin, and with it a pair of opera glasses, or there's standing room at the back of the stalls for nothing. From the box you can see the stage, and every other box in the house: who came with whom, who isn't speaking, who is passing notes behind a fan. Nobody uptown comes here for the play.
text [308c05] = 一位引座员花一个硬币就给你一间空包厢，外加一副观剧望远镜；或者正厅后排有免费的站位。从包厢里你能看到舞台，也能看到剧院里的每一间包厢：谁和谁一起来，谁不跟谁说话，谁在扇子后面递纸条。上城的人没有一个是来看戏的。
# en: A gilded theatre box at night, a student with opera glasses looking not at the stage but at the other boxes, where elegant people are whispering behind fans.
scene [83ce29] = 夜晚镀金剧院里的一间包厢，一个学生举着观剧望远镜，看的不是舞台而是其他包厢，那里衣着华贵的人正在扇子后低语。
# en: Watch the boxes
opt.left.label [7b667e] = 看包厢
# en: Through the glasses you watch a betrothal end, an alliance begin and a minister's wife pass a note to an actor. By the last act you know more about the capital than the papers do. You missed the whole play.
opt.left.say.1 [37b105] = 透过望远镜，你看着一桩婚约结束，一个同盟开始，一位大臣的夫人给一个演员递了张纸条。到最后一幕时，你对王都的了解已经超过了报纸。整出戏你一点也没看。
# en: Stand at the back of the stalls
opt.right.label [3b7037] = 站在正厅后排
# en: You leave the box to someone who wants it and stand at the back of the stalls for three acts, watching the play. It's a fine play. Your feet are sore by the end, and you're the only person in the house who could tell anyone how it ended.
opt.right.say.1 [fd4c49] = 你把包厢留给想要的人，在正厅后排站了三幕，看戏。是出好戏。结束时你脚都酸了，而全场只有你能告诉别人它是怎么结尾的。

@event theatre_stage_door
# en: The Stage Door
title [5b099a] = 后台门
# en: After the show the alley by the stage door is packed with admirers holding flowers, and one of them, a viscount's son, has been there every night for a month with a poem. The leading lady, hooded and very tired, catches your sleeve in the side doorway. "You're the academy one," she says. "Get me home without him seeing. Please."
text [cdd3cf] = 散场后，后台门边的小巷里挤满了捧着花的仰慕者，其中一位子爵的儿子已经带着一首诗在这儿守了一个月。疲惫不堪、戴着兜帽的女主角在侧门里拉住你的袖子。“你是学院来的那个吧，”她说，“送我回家，别让他看见。求你了。”
# en: A theatre's stage door in a lamp-lit alley after a show, a crowd of admirers with flowers waiting, a tired leading actress in a hooded cloak beckoning a student into a side doorway.
scene [5ac5e3] = 散场后灯光昏黄的小巷里剧院的后台门，一群捧着花的仰慕者在等候，一位披着兜帽斗篷、神情疲惫的女主角在侧门里招呼一个学生。
# en: Glamour her into someone else
opt.left.label [9f219e] = 用魅影把她变成别人
# en: A plain face, a plainer cloak: you walk her out through the middle of the crowd, past the viscount's son and his poem, and nobody looks twice. At her door she laughs for the first time all night and gives you two seats for tomorrow, which you can't use, and keep.
opt.left.say.1 [dbeb86] = 一张平常的脸，一件更平常的斗篷：你领着她从人群正中走出去，经过子爵的儿子和他的诗，没人多看一眼。到了她家门口，她终于笑了，这是她一整晚第一次笑，还给了你两张明天的票——你用不上，却留着了。
# en: The glamour slips halfway down the alley, and she's herself again, in the lamplight, in front of everybody. The viscount's son reads her the whole poem. She listens to it all with perfect manners and never speaks to you again.
opt.left.say.2 [d142c9] = 走到小巷一半，魅影滑落了，她又变回了她自己，在灯光下，在所有人面前。子爵的儿子把整首诗念给她听。她彬彬有礼地听完了，从此再也没跟你说过话。
# en: Go out front and distract him
opt.right.label [d478bb] = 到前面去拖住他
# en: You go out to the viscount's son and ask about his poem, and he reads it to you, all of it, twice, while she slips away down the alley behind him. It's a very long poem. It rhymes "heart" with "art" nine times. You hear it in your sleep.
opt.right.say.1 [3fce26] = 你走到子爵的儿子面前问起他的诗，他便念给你听，整首，念了两遍，她趁机从他身后的小巷溜走了。那首诗很长。“爱”和“在”押了九次韵。你睡梦里都在听。

@event gardens_promenade
# en: Being Seen
title [c9448f] = 被人看见
# en: {&Everyone uptown walks the Royal Gardens before luncheon, slowly, in their best, nodding to the people they've decided to nod to. Nobody looks at the flowers. Everybody looks at everybody.|The promenade again. You've learned the rules: one nod for a cousin, two for a duke, none at all for anyone in trade.|The gardens before luncheon, the gravel raked, the nods exchanged. Somebody you don't know nods to you first.}
text [ad63b8] = {&午餐前，整个上城都在皇家花园里散步，慢慢地，穿着最好的衣服，向他们决定要点头的人点头。没人看花。人人都在看人。|又是散步时间。你已经摸清了规矩：表亲点一次头，公爵点两次，做买卖的一次也不点。|午餐前的花园，碎石耙得整整齐齐，点头也交换完毕。一个你不认识的人先向你点了头。}
# en: A broad gravel walk in a formal city garden, elegant people strolling slowly in their best clothes and nodding to each other, a fountain beyond.
scene [2e1ecd] = 城市规整花园里宽阔的碎石步道，衣着华贵的人们慢慢踱步，彼此点头致意，远处是一座喷泉。
# en: Walk the promenade
opt.left.label [295f74] = 沿步道散步
# en: {&You walk the long gravel loop twice, nodding, and are nodded at by a general and a girl in a hat like a ship. Your face is tired from the effort of seeming not to try.|You nod to a duke twice and a cousin once, and only get one wrong. By the fountain someone asks your name. Your feet ache from walking slowly.|You walk, and on the second loop a lady you've never met stops you to ask whether you're the academy student everyone's talking about. You aren't, quite. You say yes. It's tiring, being someone.}
opt.left.say.1 [e27be5] = {&你沿着长长的碎石环道走了两圈，点头，一位将军和一个戴着船一样的帽子的姑娘向你点了头。为了显得毫不费力，你的脸都累了。|你向一位公爵点了两次头，向一位表亲点了一次，只错了一次。喷泉边有人问你的名字。慢慢走路让你脚都酸了。|你走着，第二圈时一位素不相识的夫人拦住你，问你是不是那个人人都在谈论的学院学生。你不完全是。你说是。当个“某人”真累。}
# en: Sit by the fountain and watch them
opt.right.label [56b1ef] = 坐在喷泉边看他们
# en: {&You sit on the fountain's rim with a pastry and watch uptown walk past nodding. It's better than the theatre. A lady's hat blows into the fountain and nobody helps her, so you do, and she cuts you dead for it.|You count nods. A duke gives forty-one before luncheon. Nobody nods at you. It's restful.|The fountain, a pastry, and the whole of uptown going round and round like the planets in an orrery. You're happy and invisible, and nobody will remember you were there.}
opt.right.say.1 [72575f] = {&你拿着一块点心坐在喷泉边沿，看上城的人点着头走过。比剧院还好看。一位夫人的帽子被风吹进喷泉，没人帮她，于是你帮了，她却当面不理你。|你数点头。午餐前一位公爵点了四十一次。没人向你点头。真清静。|喷泉，一块点心，整个上城像太阳系仪里的行星一样转了一圈又一圈。你快乐又隐形，谁也不会记得你来过。}

@event gardens_dawn_duel
# en: Wands at Noon
title [9a5089] = 正午的魔杖
# en: By the fountain, in front of half the promenade, two young nobles in shirtsleeves are about to duel with wands over an insult neither can remember. It was meant to be at dawn; both overslept. One second has fainted. The other spots your academy scarf. "You," he hisses. "Witness. It's legal with a witness." Both duellists look terrified.
text [f1f853] = 喷泉边，当着半个散步道的人，两个只穿衬衫的年轻贵族正要为一句谁也记不得的侮辱用魔杖决斗。本该在黎明决斗的，可两人都睡过了头。一位副手已经晕倒了。另一位看见了你的学院围巾。“你，”他压低嗓子，“作证。有证人就合法。”两个决斗者都吓坏了。
# en: A formal garden at midday, two rumpled young nobles in shirtsleeves facing each other across a fountain with wands raised, a nervous second, a student arriving through the hedge.
scene [142bcd] = 正午的规整花园，两个衣衫凌乱、只穿衬衫的年轻贵族隔着喷泉举杖对峙，一个紧张的副手，一个学生正从树篱里钻出来。
# en: Stand witness and let them fight
opt.left.label [c7ad8c] = 作证，让他们打
# en: You stand witness. They cast, both miss on purpose, both claim satisfaction and shake hands, and one of them faints. The other asks your name for the club book. You walk away with your heart going hard, and somebody's honour on your conscience.
opt.left.say.1 [1e2363] = 你作了证。他们施法，都故意打偏，都宣称名誉已得到满足，握了手，其中一个晕了过去。另一个问了你的名字，要记进俱乐部的簿子。你走开时心跳得厉害，良心上压着某人的名誉。
# en: Raise a ward between them
opt.right.label [09fe64] = 在他们之间升起护界
# en: You raise a ward across the fountain just as they cast, and both spells hit it and fizzle into steam. In the silence they look at each other, then at you, and start to laugh. They take you to a very late breakfast and argue all the way about who's paying.
opt.right.say.1 [9559b8] = 就在他们施法的一刹那，你在喷泉上方升起一道护界，两道咒语都撞在上面，化成一团蒸汽。一片寂静中，他们看看彼此，又看看你，笑了起来。他们拉你去吃一顿很晚的早饭，一路都在争谁请客。
# en: Your ward is a heartbeat late. Both spells cross over the fountain and hit the bronze king, who loses an ear. Everyone runs, including you. Within the hour the gardens are full of constables looking for an ear.
opt.right.say.2 [4febc2] = 你的护界晚了一拍。两道咒语越过喷泉，打中了青铜国王，国王掉了一只耳朵。人人都跑了，包括你。不到一个钟头，花园里全是在找耳朵的警员。

@event gardens_night_vine
# en: Once in Ten Years
title [d16d66] = 十年一开
# en: In the Royal Gardens' great hothouse, a southern vine that flowers once in ten years has opened overnight, and the queue to see it goes twice round the glasshouse. An under-gardener at the door is quietly selling cuttings to anyone who asks the right way. It shouldn't grow this far north. Nothing in here should.
text [463886] = 皇家花园的大温室里，一株十年才开一次花的南方藤蔓昨夜开了，看花的队伍绕温室排了两圈。门口一个园丁助手正悄悄把插条卖给懂得怎么开口的人。它本不该长在这么北的地方。这里头的东西都不该。
# en: A tall glass hothouse full of tropical plants, a vast pale vine in flower, a long quiet queue of people waiting their turn to see it, a gardener with a lantern.
scene [97bbbe] = 高大的玻璃温室里满是热带植物，一株巨大的淡色藤蔓正在开花，一条安静的长队等着轮到自己看，一位园丁提着灯。
# en: Queue to see it
opt.left.label [59d1b7] = 排队去看
# en: You queue in the wet heat for three hours, and when you get there it's a single white flower the size of a dinner plate, and it smells like the sea at night. Nobody in the queue speaks for a while after. You come out light-headed and very glad.
opt.left.say.1 [18b9b7] = 你在湿热里排了三个小时，轮到你时，只是一朵餐盘大的白花，闻起来像夜里的海。队伍里的人好一会儿都没说话。你出来时头晕目眩，满心欢喜。
# en: Buy a cutting from the under-gardener
opt.right.label [965eb2] = 向园丁助手买一根插条
# en: Behind a palm the under-gardener sells you a cutting wrapped in wet moss, and tells you how to keep it, and how to brew from it if it doesn't take. It doesn't take. You brew it, and the brew is extraordinary, and slightly illegal.
opt.right.say.1 [4881a1] = 在一棵棕榈后面，园丁助手卖给你一根裹着湿苔藓的插条，告诉你怎么养活它，养不活的话怎么用它调药。它没活。你用它调了药，药效惊人，还有点违法。

@event gardens_thornwick_dare
# en: The Bronze King's Horse
title [3d92d0] = 青铜国王的马
# en: Under the bronze statue of the old king on his rearing horse, a knot of students in Thornwick colours spot your Lumenhold scarf. "Lumenhold," says their leader. "We've heard you can animate things. Make the horse walk. One lap of the fountain. Or we tell everyone at Thornwick you couldn't."
text [4b46df] = 老国王骑着腾跃战马的青铜像下，一群穿荆木学院校色的学生看见了你的流明堡围巾。“流明堡的，”领头的说，“听说你们会让东西活过来。让这匹马走起来。绕喷泉一圈。不然我们就告诉全荆木你办不到。”
# en: A formal garden with a great bronze statue of a king on a rearing horse, a group of smirking students in Thornwick colours pointing at it and at a student in Lumenhold colours.
scene [8970d0] = 规整花园里一座国王骑着腾跃战马的巨大青铜像，一群穿荆木校色的学生得意地指着雕像和一个穿流明堡校色的学生。
# en: Make the horse walk
opt.left.label [093f76] = 让马走起来
# en: The bronze horse shakes its mane, steps down off its plinth with the king still aboard and walks one stately lap of the fountain while uptown screams. It climbs back up and rears again, exactly as before. The Thornwick students say nothing at all, and buy you an ice.
opt.left.say.1 [b0457d] = 青铜马甩了甩鬃毛，驮着国王走下底座，庄重地绕喷泉走了一圈，上城的人尖叫连连。它爬回原处，又和原来一模一样地腾跃起来。荆木的学生们一句话也没说，请你吃了一份冰。
# en: The horse lifts one hoof, thinks better of it and puts it down on a flowerbed. The Thornwick students cheer. A gardener presents you with the bill for the tulips.
opt.left.say.2 [dc871a] = 马抬起一只蹄子，想了想，又把它放在了花坛上。荆木的学生们欢呼起来。一位园丁把郁金香的账单递给了你。
# en: Refuse, and make them laugh instead
opt.right.label [250845] = 拒绝，换个法子逗他们笑
# en: You tell them the horse is on strike for better oats, and do the horse's voice, and the king's, and by the end they're laughing too hard to remember the dare. They'll still tell Thornwick you wouldn't. They'll also tell them you were funny.
opt.right.say.1 [c7afe5] = 你告诉他们这匹马正为更好的燕麦罢工，还学马的声音、学国王的声音，到最后他们笑得忘了打赌这回事。他们照样会告诉荆木你不敢。他们也会告诉荆木你很逗。

@event bridges_lamplighters
# en: The Lamplighters' Guild
title [075790] = 点灯人行会
# en: {&At dusk the lamplighters' guild comes out on the bridges. Tonight the oldest lamp on the middle bridge, the one they say the guild's founder lit, keeps going out the moment it's lit, and the guild-master is offering a coin to anyone who can make it stay.|Fog off the sea tonight, and the lamplighters' guild has to light the bridges early and fast or the barges will hit the piers. The guild-master is short-handed and shouting for anyone with two arms.|The lamplighters' guild has a boat, it turns out, for the lamps under the arches that can only be reached from the water. Tonight its oarsman is sick, and the guild-master is looking hopefully at the crowd on the bank.}
text [26ce4e] = {&黄昏时分，点灯人行会的人走上桥。今晚中桥上最老的那盏灯——据说是行会创始人点亮的——刚点着就灭，会长悬赏一枚硬币，看谁能让它一直亮着。|今晚海上起了雾，点灯人行会得提早飞快地点亮几座桥，不然驳船会撞上桥墩。会长人手不够，冲任何有两条胳膊的人大喊。|原来点灯人行会有一条船，专门去点那些只能从水上够到的桥拱下的灯。今晚划船的病了，会长满怀希望地看着岸上的人群。}
# en: Three old stone bridges over a river at dusk, lamplighters in guild coats at work along the parapets, a guild-master in a cocked hat calling to a student for help.
scene [7e4d5e] = 黄昏时河上三座古老的石桥，穿行会外套的点灯人在桥栏边忙碌，一位戴三角帽的行会会长正招呼一个学生来帮忙。
# en: Lend the guild a hand
opt.left.label [4670d8] = 给行会搭把手
# en: {&You stand by the old lamp all evening, relighting it and shielding it, and learn at last that a gull has been nesting in the cowl and fanning it out. You move the gull. The lamp stays lit. The guild-master pays you, and the gull bites you.|You run the length of all three bridges in the fog with a pole and a lantern, and the lamps go on just ahead of the barges. The guild-master pays you and gives you a guild armband to keep. Your lungs burn.|You row the guild's boat from arch to arch while a lamplighter stands in the bow and lights the lamps under the bridges, one by one, upside down in the water too. Your hands blister. The pay is honest.}
opt.left.say.1 [6afb24] = {&你在那盏老灯旁站了一整晚，一次次点燃它、护着它，终于发现是一只海鸥在灯罩里筑巢，把火扇灭了。你挪走了海鸥。灯一直亮着。会长付了钱，海鸥咬了你一口。|你扛着杆子提着灯，在雾里跑遍三座桥，灯总在驳船前面一步亮起。会长付了钱，还给了你一条行会臂章留着。你的肺火辣辣的。|你划着行会的船一个桥拱一个桥拱地去，船头的点灯人把桥下的灯一盏盏点亮，水里也倒映着一盏盏。你的手磨出了水泡。挣的是干净钱。}
# en: Buy chestnuts and watch
opt.right.label [b3aea8] = 买包栗子看着
# en: {&You lean on the parapet with a paper of hot chestnuts and watch the lamps go on one by one along all three bridges until the river is full of their reflections. The old lamp goes out twice, and comes back twice, and the crowd cheers it.|You watch from the bank as the lamps come on in the fog, a string of soft gold, and eat your chestnuts slowly, to make them last.|You watch the guild's boat go from arch to arch, lighting the river from underneath. It's the most beautiful thing in the capital, and it's free, except for the chestnuts.}
opt.right.say.1 [74e6f0] = {&你捧着一纸包热栗子靠在桥栏上，看着三座桥的灯一盏接一盏亮起，直到河里满是倒影。那盏老灯灭了两次，又亮了两次，人群为它欢呼。|你在岸上看着灯在雾里一盏盏亮起，像一串柔和的金子，慢慢吃着栗子，好让它们吃得久一点。|你看着行会的船从一个桥拱划到另一个，从下面把河照亮。这是王都最美的东西，而且免费——栗子除外。}

@event bridges_busking
# en: An Empty Hat on the Span
title [256a44] = 桥上的空帽子
# en: {&The bridges are the capital's stage: a fire-eater, a fiddler, a man with a dog that counts. There's a free stretch of parapet between a juggler and a woman selling ribbons. You have a wand, an empty hat and a bridge full of people.|The bridges are thick with people, a ship's in and its crew are spending. The only free stretch of parapet is beside the ribbon-seller, who looks at your wand with suspicion.|The juggler on the middle bridge is packing up, and offers you his pitch, the best on the river, for the rest of the light.}
text [d722b7] = {&这几座桥是王都的舞台：一个吞火人，一个提琴手，一个带着会数数的狗的男人。一个杂耍艺人和一个卖缎带的女人之间空着一段桥栏。你有一根魔杖、一顶空帽子和一整座桥的人。|桥上人挤人，有条船进了港，船员们正在花钱。唯一空着的桥栏在卖缎带的女人旁边，她狐疑地看着你的魔杖。|中桥上的杂耍艺人正在收摊，他把自己的位置让给你，那是河上最好的位置，到天黑为止都归你。}
# en: A crowded old stone bridge, buskers and hawkers along the parapets, a student with an upturned hat performing street magic for a gathering crowd.
scene [36844e] = 拥挤的古老石桥，桥栏边是街头艺人和小贩，一个学生摆着翻过来的帽子，给越聚越多的人表演街头魔术。
# en: Put on a show
opt.left.label [bc2111] = 来一场表演
# en: {&You make birds out of lamp-smoke and send them round the crowd's heads, and the hat fills with coins and one button. The juggler shakes your hand. The bridges have a new act.|You make a smoke dragon that chases the man with the counting dog round the parapet. The dog counts it: one. The sailors roar, and the hat fills.|On the juggler's pitch the crowd is three deep, and somebody's grandmother asks for a dragon. You make her one. The hat's too heavy to lift with one hand.}
opt.left.say.1 [b3f3de] = {&你用灯烟变出一群鸟，让它们绕着观众的头顶飞，帽子里装满了硬币，外加一颗纽扣。杂耍艺人和你握了手。桥上有了一个新节目。|你变出一条烟龙，追着那个带着会数数的狗的男人绕桥栏跑。狗数了数它：一。水手们哄堂大笑，帽子满了。|在杂耍艺人的位置上，观众围了三层，有人的祖母要看一条龙。你给她变了一条。帽子重得一只手都提不起来。}
# en: {&Your smoke birds come out as smoke. The crowd drifts to the fiddler. The juggler gives you one of his coins, out of pity, which is worse than nothing.|The dragon comes out the size of a sparrow and flies into the river. A sailor laughs. Nobody else does.|The wind takes your smoke the wrong way, all of it, into the ribbon-seller's face. She moves her whole stall away from you, very pointedly.}
opt.left.say.2 [9249f3] = {&你的烟鸟出来只是烟。观众散到提琴手那边去了。杂耍艺人出于同情给了你一枚他的硬币，这比什么都没有还糟。|那条龙出来只有麻雀大，一头扎进了河里。一个水手笑了。别人都没笑。|风把你的烟吹错了方向，全吹到了卖缎带的女人脸上。她把整个摊子搬得离你远远的，意思再明白不过。}
# en: Watch the professionals
opt.right.label [8026af] = 看看行家怎么做
# en: {&You watch the juggler work the crowd for an hour: where he looks, when he drops a ball on purpose, how the hat goes round. It's better than a lecture, and less fun than doing it.|You watch the man with the counting dog. The dog, you're fairly sure, is counting him.|You stand at the back and study the fire-eater's patter, line by line. You'll use it one day. Tonight you just get cold.}
opt.right.say.1 [19bee0] = {&你看了一个小时杂耍艺人怎么拿捏观众：他看哪里，什么时候故意掉一个球，帽子怎么传下去。比听课还有用，却没有自己上场好玩。|你看着那个带着会数数的狗的男人。你几乎可以肯定，是狗在数他。|你站在后面，一句一句琢磨吞火人的台词。总有一天你会用上的。今晚你只是冻着了。}

@event bridges_span_duel
# en: The Middle of the Middle Bridge
title [0e82fb] = 中桥的正中
# en: There's an old custom on the middle bridge: at night, a wand drawn on the very middle span is a challenge, and the duel is a duel of lights, first to put out the other's. A young duellist in a sailor's coat, off a ship in the harbour, is standing there with a wand alight, waiting for anyone. The crowd has noticed your scarf. "Academy," someone says. Everybody steps back.
text [58f053] = 中桥上有个老规矩：夜里在正中的那一跨拔出魔杖，就是下战书，决斗是比光，先把对方的光熄灭者胜。一个穿水手外套、从港里船上下来的年轻决斗者站在那里，魔杖亮着，等着随便什么人。人群注意到了你的围巾。“学院的，”有人说。大家都往后退了一步。
# en: The middle of an old stone bridge at night, lamps lit along both parapets, a young duellist in a sailor's coat with a raised wand of light facing a student, a small crowd gathering.
scene [8905ab] = 夜晚古老石桥的正中，两侧桥栏的灯都亮着，一个穿水手外套的年轻决斗者举着一根发光的魔杖面对一个学生，一小群人聚了过来。
# en: Draw your wand on the span
opt.left.label [9c314a] = 在桥心拔出魔杖
# en: Your light and theirs, a long white minute over the dark river, and then theirs gutters and goes out. The sailor bows, lights their wand from yours, which is the custom, and walks off without a word. The crowd talks about it for the rest of the break.
opt.left.say.1 [ba8836] = 你的光和对方的光，在漆黑的河面上对峙了长长的一分钟，白晃晃的，然后对方的光摇曳着熄灭了。水手鞠了一躬，按规矩用你的光点亮自己的魔杖，一言不发地走了。整个假期剩下的日子，人们都在谈论这件事。
# en: Theirs is brighter, and steadier, and yours goes out with a little sound like a sigh. They bow and offer you their light, which is the custom. The crowd is kind about it. The sailors on the quay won't be.
opt.left.say.2 [f45f54] = 对方的光更亮，也更稳，你的光像一声叹息似的熄灭了。对方鞠了一躬，按规矩把光借给你。人群对此很宽厚。码头上的水手们可不会。
# en: Walk round by the old bridge
opt.right.label [390074] = 绕道从老桥走
# en: You walk round by the old bridge, the long way, as somebody in the crowd mutters "academy" in a disappointed voice. Behind you, the sailor's wand stays lit on the middle span till midnight, waiting for anyone.
opt.right.say.1 [208b85] = 你绕道从老桥走，远远地绕了一圈，人群里有人失望地嘟囔了一句“学院的”。在你身后，水手的魔杖在中桥正中一直亮到午夜，等着随便什么人。

@event bridges_mudlark
# en: The Mudlark's Find
title [464523] = 淘泥人的发现
# en: At low tide the mudlarks come out under the middle bridge with sacks and sieves, and an old mudlark calls you down the river stairs to show you today's best: a sealed green bottle with a letter inside, and a ring with a name and a street engraved in it. "The bottle's for sale," she says. "The ring wants taking home, and my knees won't do the hill."
text [d26c57] = 退潮时，淘泥人带着麻袋和筛子来到中桥底下，一个年老的淘泥人把你叫下河边台阶，给你看今天最好的收获：一只装着信的密封绿瓶子，还有一枚刻着人名和街名的戒指。“瓶子卖，”她说，“戒指得有人送回家，我的膝盖爬不动那座坡。”
# en: A river at low tide under an old stone bridge, an old mudlark woman with a sack holding up a muddy sealed bottle and a ring to a student on the river stairs.
scene [a92971] = 古老石桥下退潮的河滩，一位背着麻袋的年老淘泥女人在河边台阶上向一个学生举起一只沾满泥的密封瓶子和一枚戒指。
# en: Buy the bottle
opt.left.label [19c560] = 买下瓶子
# en: You break the seal on the bridge. The letter is a hundred years old, from a lighthouse keeper's wife to a sailor who never came back, and it's so plainly in love that you have to stop reading twice. You keep it. You'll never find out how it ended.
opt.left.say.1 [08d956] = 你在桥上打开封口。信写于一百年前，是一个灯塔看守人的妻子写给一个再也没回来的水手的，爱意直白得你中途停下了两次。你留下了它。你永远不会知道结局如何。
# en: Take the ring home for her
opt.right.label [9202f0] = 替她把戒指送回去
# en: The street is at the top of the steepest hill in uptown. The old woman who opens the door turns the ring over and sits down on her own step. It was her mother's, lost off the bridge sixty years ago. She gives you tea. Your legs ache all the way back down.
opt.right.say.1 [2d25f4] = 那条街在上城最陡的山坡顶上。开门的老妇人把戒指翻过来，在自家台阶上坐了下来。那是她母亲的，六十年前从桥上掉了下去。她请你喝了茶。下坡的一路上你的腿都在疼。
